# The vault's power-off, as a ROOT-OWNED unit that does not depend on the agent
# being alive. This is path 2 and 3 of the three independent power-off paths;
# gs_wake_agent's own `finally` is path 1.
#
# SIGTERM THE JOB'S GROUP FIRST. Every tool in this toolchain installs a
# SIGTERM handler and its `finally` blocks are what erase the plan files and
# the wallet-password scratch file. gs_console's job watchdog records what a
# straight kill cost: "a timed-out run left all of it on disk."
[Unit]
Description=GhostSpiral wake: stop the job cleanly, then power off
DefaultDependencies=no
Before=shutdown.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# NO CORE DUMPS. Set here for consistency with gs-wake-agent.service, NOT
# because this unit holds a secret -- it runs `systemctl kill` and
# `systemctl poweroff` and has no key material, no wallet password and no
# plan in its address space.
#
# THE AGENT UNIT'S COMMENT WAS COPY-PASTED HERE AND THEN PATCHED, and the
# patch left a sentence contradicting itself: "the secret arrives in the
# ENVIRONMENT (nothing itself, but it shares the agent's cgroup)", followed
# by a driven measurement -- "a SIGABRT wrote a 5 MB core and the secret was
# in it" -- that is a measurement of the AGENT, not of this. A parenthetical
# saying "nothing itself" inside a sentence about a secret is how a reader
# comes to believe this unit handles one.
#
# The real reason to keep the line: a shutdown path that dumps core writes a
# file at the exact moment the machine is going down, when nothing is left
# running to erase it -- and it costs nothing to forbid. See
# gs-wake-agent.service for the case where a dump genuinely does carry the
# secret, and for why a Python statement cannot be early enough to prevent it.
LimitCORE=0
# Give the agent's process group a chance to run its cleanup, then insist.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'systemctl kill --signal=SIGTERM gs-wake-agent.service 2>/dev/null || true; sleep 20; systemctl kill --signal=SIGKILL gs-wake-agent.service 2>/dev/null || true'
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl --no-block poweroff
